# Lecture 09C

## Mitotic Crossover

## Chapter 8

## Dr. Neil Voss

## November 3, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Connect DNA repair to crossover and loss of heterozygosity.
- Explain how one altered cell can produce twin spots.
- Apply these ideas to cancer and studies of gene function.

=== layout: one-panel

# Mitotic Cross-Over

@body

- Recombination can also occur in dividing body cells.
- DNA damage or replication problems can initiate repair before the next mitosis.
- A crossover between homologs is an uncommon repair outcome.
- Many cell divisions give rare events a chance to become visible.

@citation

Johnson and Jasin (2000): https://doi.org/10.1093/emboj/19.13.3398

=== layout: two-panels

# Repair Starts with a Matching Copy

@left

- DNA replication produces sister chromatids.
- The sister is a useful template for repairing DNA breaks.
- Most repair finishes without a crossover.
- A crossover with the other homolog can change allele combinations.

@right

![A damaged chromatid and two possible matching copies](assets/lect09c-mitotic_crossover/repair_templates.svg)

@citation

Johnson and Jasin (2000): https://doi.org/10.1093/emboj/19.13.3398

=== layout: two-panels

# Loss of Heterozygosity

@left

- Start with an A/a cell.
- After replication and crossover, one segregation pattern gives *A/A* and *a/a* daughters.
- *LOH*: instead of two allele versions, the cell now has only one version.
- Here, both daughters still have two chromosome copies.

@right

![Two diploid daughters become homozygous at a distal marker](assets/lect09c-mitotic_crossover/loh_outcome.svg)

@citation

Hagstrom and Dryja (1999): https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.96.6.2952

@notes

The diagram shows one possible segregation after an exchange between the centromere and A. The next figure also shows the outcome that retains heterozygosity. Markers distal to the exchange can become homozygous. LOH can also arise by mechanisms other than crossing over.

=== layout: one-panel

# Mitotic Recombination and Twin Spots

@body

![Lecture diagram](assets/lect09b-tetrads_1_introduction/image_001.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel


# Twin Spots After Mitotic Recombination

@body

In a y sn^+^ / y^+^ sn _Drosophila_ female, most of the body is wild type.

Patches sometimes show yellow color or singed bristles. Adjacent yellow and singed patches form *twin spots*.

![Single yellow spot, twin spot, and single singed spot](assets/lect09_tetrad_concepts/twin_spots.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# One Event Can Make a Patch

@left

- Descendants of one altered cell form a *clone*.
- A *mosaic* contains cells with different genotypes in one individual.
- Earlier events often have more time to make larger patches.
- Patch size also depends on cell division and survival.

@right

![Two daughter lineages produce neighboring clones](assets/lect09c-mitotic_crossover/clonal_patches.svg)

@citation

Graf (1995): https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01929364

=== layout: one-panel

# LOH Can Supply a Second Hit

@body

For a tumor-suppressor gene, let *R* work and *r* be a loss-of-function allele.

| Cell | Genotype | Working Copies |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Before LOH | R/r | 1 |
| One daughter | r/r | 0 |
| Reciprocal daughter | R/R | 2 |

- The r allele was already present; another point mutation is not required.
- LOH at *RB1* can contribute to retinoblastoma, a cancer of the retina.

@citation

Cavenee et al. (1983): https://doi.org/10.1038/305779a0

=== layout: two-panels

# Twin Spots Can Test Gene Function

@left

- Researchers can induce recombination and label both daughter lineages.
- Compare clone growth, survival, or cell shape within the same tissue.
- If one clone is smaller, what could explain it?

Slower division or more cell death are possibilities.

@right

![Paired clones allow comparison within one tissue](assets/lect09c-mitotic_crossover/clonal_patches.svg)

@citation

Griffin et al. (2009): https://doi.org/10.1038/nmeth.1349

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- Repair usually preserves the sequence; crossovers can change allele combinations.
- Mitotic crossover can cause LOH while chromosome copy number stays the same.
- Altered daughter cells can grow into genetically different patches.
- These clones connect recessive alleles to cancer and studies of gene function.

